rategies as you want  and as long as the environment is on  they will all be evaluated  and if any one of them return true for  the given context then the feature will  be on for that user  so that means that if you have a gradual  rollout set to zero like we have here  then it will be on or no users  but we will also go ahead and check the  user id strategy  and since my user id is user 1 and i  pass that in  i still get access to the feature so  every strategy is evaluated  and that makes it really flexible  because you can add as many strategies  as you want that constrain on different  properties  and you can even create your own custom  strategies and provide the  implementation in code  so that you can create strategies that  don't really exist in the system out of  the box but that you need in the context  that you're operating in  so if we open up the sidebar here we can  take a look at the built-in strategies  and we've already been through two of  them  and the ip and host strategy work very  similarly to the user id strategy  so if we add the ip strategy  we need to provide an ip here  and once we've added that and we saved  the strategy  we can see that the execution plan is  updated  but now we need to take this ip and we  need to add it to our  code so let's go ahead and remove our  user id because  we want to see that we don't have access  to the feature anymore  if we jump back to our application  now we don't have access to the chatbot  but if we go ahead and take this ip  address  and we head back to the code  now we can add a remote address property  here  and we put in the ip address  and let's go back to the browser  and now we once again have access via  the remote address strategy so all of  the strategies have been evaluated  and this final one returned true  so we get access to the feature because  our ip address matches the value that is  put in here  all right so now we've gotten our feed a  little bit bet with feature toggles and  we've seen how we can use strategies to  target different segments of the user  base  so what i want to focus on next is  experimentation  and sometimes as developers we want to  develop different versions of a feature  and try it out so we can determine which  one performs better  and  that's a process that keeps the users  close to us  and that's important because we don't  want to just take a feature and run with  it and develop something that we think  is right we want to continuously  validate that we're actually building  something  that gives real value to our users  and in order to do that experimentation  and a b testing can be quite powerful  tools that we can use to figure out  which one performs better  and i'm going to show you how to do this  in unleash  but before i do i just want to mention  quickly how we think about working with  other services because we don't have any  built-in analytics in unleash and one of  the reasons for that is actually back to  our philosophy  because we believe that  there's a lot of software being written  today and there's a lot of great work  that others do  and  for example analytics you have great  providers that provide a world-class  analytics experience  and we believe that it's our job to  make as many integration points  and provide as flexible as system as  possible so that you can use the great  work of others  and combine services to gain a better  experience  so  we're not really focusing on  implementing analytics in the  traditional sense when it comes to  experimentation  we instead focus on  giving you the option of taking some  provider that you're probably already  using today  and pairing that with data from unleash  and then you can send that off to your  analytics provider and do the  uh necessary  analysis on the data  so with that said let's jump into it and  see how we can do some a b testing with  unleash  okay so here we are back in the unleash  application and we've done a little bit  of cleanup on the chatbot page toggle  as you can see we removed some of these  strategies that were here before and  what we're left with is the gradual  rollout that's rolling out to 100  percent of our users  so  we're going to use this feature toggle  and set up an a b experiment  and in order to do that we'll need to  learn about a new concept in unleash  called variance  so we'll navigate to the variance tab  and we'll go ahead and add a couple  we'll have variant a here  and then we'll add another one  and now we basically have the setup that  we need  we still need to do some work on the  cold side of things but  before we do let's try and understand  the weight distribution here  so you can see that each variant has a  weight distribution of 50 percent  and this means that  50 of the users that are visiting our  site and that get access to this feature  will get variant a  and 50 will get variant b  so let's take a very concrete example  and say we have a hundred users visiting  we have a gradual rollout that says that  everyone visiting will get access  that means that 50 users will get  variant a  and 50 users will get variant b  but let's change the strategy just to  see how that affects the  weight distribution  so we'll change this now to 50  and we'll head back to the variance  let's take the same example again we  have a hundred users visiting our site  we only have 50 percent rollout so that  means that we only have 50 users that  get access to the chatbot feature  and since we have a 50 50 weight  distribution that means that of those 50  users  25 users will get variant a and 25 users  will get variant b  so it's all relative to the amount of  people that get access to the feature  toggle  so you can think of strategies sort of  like a gatekeeper that lets people  through or not and then the variants can  be thought of as pathways that we send  the people that get through the gates on  after they are let through  so currently we have variant a and  variant b which means that there are two  possible pathways for the people that  are lit true  and we could easily add more pathways if  we wanted to  but hopefully that makes sense  and we'll now go ahead and implement the  code side of this  so before we jump to the code let's head  back to the strategies  and we'll make sure that we set the  gradual rollout back to 100  and with that we can check that we  propagate this to the application  and we do so let's head into the code  now  and we'll grab the use variant hook  from the unleash proxy client library  and then we'll do variant  equals use variant  and we'll  put in the feature toggle name  so with that  we can go ahead and log this out here  we'll open up the console  and we can see here that  we got the variant a which corresponds  to with the variant name that we added  or one of the variants  you see  here  we have the variant a here so we  automatically got one of the variants  that we defined  now  we also have the enable true here which  means that we don't really need to use  the use flag hook anymore because we get  both of the data properties  in this object so  we'll go back to the code  and we'll remove the enabled  and then we'll say  variant enabled here  and we'll pass the variant as a prop  here to this app with chatbot component  and let's accept it here  what we want to do now based on this  variant  we are going to render a different  welcome message in the chatbot  so in order to do that  let's create a new function  inside of the  configuration here  and we will create  a  getconfig method  which is going to take a welcome message  and it's going to return  the configuration that we put in here  but the initial message is going to be  the  welcome message  so we'll export  the config function  and then we'll head back to app.js  and we will  rename this  okay so let's remove the use flag here  since we're not using that anymore  and we'll scroll down to our  app with chatbot component where we are  accepting the variant as a parameter  now we're gonna do  config equals  config  and then we want to paste in the message  here and i've already prepared that so  we skip some typing  but what we want to do is we want to  check the variant name  like we're doing here  and if the variant name is variant a  then we  want to have this welcoming message  otherwise  we want to have this welcoming message  so we'll hit save  and we'll pass this into  our get config  and we are passing the config here so  everything is as it should be  with that we can go ahead and open up  the browser again  and you can see that we get the  welcome message here  if we refresh  and we open up the chatbot again  here we have the same message so that's  working fine  so  let's see if we can trigger  this message here as well because  right now we have been given a variant  and that variant is not going to change  because we  have passed in the user id here  and that will ensure that the variant is  sticky as well so we'll get the same  experience  whenever we open the website  but what we could do in order to  trigger or force  a change is we can take this user id  here  and we can go back to our  unleash  application  and inside of variant b  we can go ahead and add an overwrite  and you see here we have a context field  called user id  let's put our user in there  and we'll hit save  and you can see now we have an override  on this variant  so if we go back to our application now  and we refresh  now we can see that we have another  message and that's in fact the message  that we  provided as an alternative  so here we're using variants to change  the messaging in the chatbot when we  launch it  but that's a simple example and if we  take a look at the code all we're really  doing is we're checking the variant name  and then  doing something based on that so here  we're changing the text but it could  easily be  rendering a different component  or  changing something within another  component it's really up to you and what  your experiment entails and how you want  to go about it but it's as flexible as  it needs to be to  cover the use cases that you could have  and here we only have two variants but  you could easily have three or four or  five  and  that's up to you and your your ex  now let's talk about analytics  i mentioned briefly before that we  prefer to defer to world-class analytics  providers  in order to allow you to integrate with  providers that you most likely are  already using today  i wanted to show you how you can use  unleash  together with your analytics provider  and create a trail that makes it so that  you can track  a given variant through your application  so i'm going to show an example with  google analytics  and i've already installed the  react google analytics package here so  let's go ahead and  initialize it  and then we'll take a look at how we  could do this  so what we'll do is we'll import  react ga  from  react  ea  and then we'll go ahead and initialize  it so  inside of our application we can put it  in a use effect  we initialize it with the  following  parameters and then this would be your  unique google account key  all right so let's do a few more changes  first of all we'll create a variable for  our user id  and we'll put the user id in here  and then  we will  place that in here  and we'll replace the value here as well  and then what we'll do  is we'll go down to our app with chatbot  and we'll go ahead and add a user  picture as well  and we'll make it dependent on the  variant  and we'll save variant  name  and variant enabled  let's do react  ea  and we'll use an event here  and then we can send an event to  google analytics  so we can say label  application loaded  and  let's go ahead and add the variant  that's going to be the variant name  and then we'll give it an action  it's a required property  we'll say  application loaded with  and give it the variant  name  and we'll also want to give the user id  here  that means we need to go up in our  parent component  we need to pass in the user id  here  and we'll accept that as a parameter  and now we have the ability to send an  event  to google analytics  and that event  includes the user id and the variant  name  and that means that now you have your  baseline because you have the user id  and you know which variant that user id  loaded up with  so if you're tracking  more actions on your site  for example if you want the user to  convert or in this case let's say we  wanted to check whether or not the user  is interacting with the chatbot based on  the message  we could go ahead and add more events  with the user id and the variant name in  other places of the application where it  makes sense  and we know that  the action has been taken by the user  with this variant okay so that's the way  we can track the user across the  application  if that's what you want to do  all right so i want to show you a little  bit about the metrics as well because  one of the coolest things about feature  toggles is that you can see  the updates live and you can see who is  getting access to what feature or how  many people are getting access to a  feature  so when you wire up the sdks and you put  up the api key  you'll start sending metrics based on  the environment that that api key is  configured for so i currently have a  script running that's calling against  the api with an api key that's enabled  for development  and we currently have the  strategies set to a hundred percent so  we have the gradual rollout  set to 100 so everyone who  is asking against this api  is getting  enabled back for this feature  so let's go ahead and update this  we'll set the gradual rollout to 50  percent  and  we head back to our application now  we're going to be able to see as this  updates that more and more people are  getting denied  the access to the feature and it's going  to creep back  towards the 50  mark over time  and that's how you can be sure that when  you're setting a gradual rollout  or you're setting a strategy  configuration  that you expect is going to  be enabled for  a subset of your users you could use the  metrics to verify that that is in fact  happening  and now we can see that we're slowly  making our way towards the 50 mark in  more and more of the users that are  asking for access towards the api are  getting denied  which is what we expect  right so we talked about metrics a  little bit and besides that there are  some other methods for monitoring that  you can do in unleash  and if you remember we talked to ebar  earlier about technical depth and we  take it really seriously because you  have to be able to clean up your code  and remove feature flags as you go along  to have a sustainable workflow when  you're working with feature toggles  so  i'm here in the demo instance because it  has a lot more  feature flags available and i want to  show you the  health dashboard that we built  which is sort of a work in progress but  we have an early version of it ready  and we can head over to the project  and this is a demo instance that anyone  can log in and use so there will be a  lot of  projects here and a lot of data  because it's kind of a sandbox that  anyone can use and try out unleash  so  if we head over to the health section  here you see we have a health rating and  we have a toggle report  and then we have all of the feature  totals in this project down in the  overview here  and  you get some information here  basically the name of the toggle when it  was last seen which is when did we last  register and sdk  asking for this feature toggle  and so it's if it's never seen then  you've never connected up an sdk and  started to use this feature toggle but  if you do have a value here then you  know that it's eight days since this  last was in use which might indicate  that it was used in a  local environment and not in the  production environment because if you're  in production you would expect it to be  continuously  asked for  and then you have like you can see some  more information when it's created  and there is an expired column which  basically  based on the  type of feature toggle you create we use  some internal data  to calculate when it's expired and for  release toggle that's about 40 days  because  that's what the research indicates that  should be the lifetime or life  expectancy of a toggle like that  so you can use this dashboard to get an  overview over  toggles that might be  ripe for cleaning up and it can help you  in the effort to  clean up your toggles and  get rid of some of the technical depth  all right  that's it for the course  and i want to thank you so much for  tuning in and giving me the opportunity  to explain this concept  and i hope it's been a valuable lesson  in terms of understanding how to work  with feature toggles and how you can use  them to simplify your workflow  and run experiments within your code  now if you're wondering where you can  find unleash we're on social media  obviously but the most important part is  the code that's on github  we are after all an open source  organization  and you can go to  the unleash repositories and you can  browse through all of the code  and if you want to contribute there are  opportunities for that as well if you  have any other questions you can tune in  to our open slack channel we'll be happy  to help you along if you want to get  started using feature toggles  now if you want to know where you can  reach me i'm on twitter under at f  osberg  or you can find me at my youtube channel  which is called frederick osborne coding  lessons here on youtube  and with that i hope you have a really  great day
 

